          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "**What the sources say about measurement**\n\nThe retrieved material defines the two numbers. Blood pressure is written as systolic over diastolic, both measured in millimeters of mercury. Systolic is the pressure when the heart contracts. Diastolic is the pressure between beats (*Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, General clinical reference).\n\nThe sources I could search do not explain how the cuff is inflated and deflated or how the readings are picked up. I can't answer that part from this corpus, so a nurse or clinician is the best source for the step-by-step method.\n\n**Why more than one reading is needed**\n\n- **Pressure varies all the time.** It changes with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep and time of day. It is usually lowest during sleep and rises before waking. A single reading captures one moment (*Hypertension (high blood pressure)*).\n- **A single high reading is not a diagnosis.** Guidance says diagnosis uses the average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, Major cardiology society guidance).\n- **Measurement setup matters.** The guideline notes that certain positioning and technique factors can push a reading up, each by an amount comparable to a whole stage of the classification. The passage I retrieved was cut off before naming them, so I can't list them from this source.\n- **Office readings can mislead.** Home readings or 24-hour ambulatory monitoring can show two patterns that clinic readings miss. In white-coat hypertension, office readings are raised but out-of-office readings are not. In masked hypertension, the reverse is true and risk can be underestimated (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*).\n\n**What to discuss with a clinician**\n\nIf you have had a high reading, ask whether home or 24-hour monitoring would help confirm it, and ask how your readings should be taken at home.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
